}

NOTES: Build one frustum as a reusable THREE.Group (LineSegments for the 8 black edges + 2 face diagonals, a small black sphere or Points sprite for the optical center) and instance it five times along X. Use LineBasicMaterial with linewidth fallback — for the heavy stroke weight in the source, prefer thin tube meshes (CylinderGeometry along each edge, radius ~0.012) or Line2/LineMaterial from examples so thickness survives on all platforms. Red arrows: build from a flat ExtrudeGeometry or a BoxGeometry shaft plus ConeGeometry head, MeshBasicMaterial color 0xFF0000, unlit. Default camera: standard isometric axonometric pose via OrthographicCamera so the five copies stay exactly equal in size (no perspective shrink), zoom to fit the ~11-unit strip width at heightFraction 0.55. The animation should translate a single highlighted 'active' camera along the baseline while the five static ghosts remain as the strip; on loop, reset to x = -4.8. Slider and animation share the same lateralOffset state so dragging the slider scrubs the animation. In 'Projected optical flow' mode, fade the 3-D strip to ~15% opacity, bring an OrthographicCamera or a 2D overlay canvas to the front showing the image rectangle filled white with a thin black border, and draw the projected point positions plus flow arrows recomputed each frame from u = f·X/Z with the current t_x. Keep flow-arrow heads screen-space-constant so short far-point vectors remain legible.

