CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a horizontal strip in which the same wireframe camera appears five times, evenly spaced across a white field and interleaved with thick red arrows. Each camera is a viewing frustum seen from behind and slightly below: a solid black dot at the bottom marks the projection center, four straight edges rise from it to the corners of a wide rectangle standing for the image plane, and lines cross that rectangle to its lower corners, so the pyramid reads as pointing up and away from the viewer. The five copies match exactly in size and orientation. Red arrows run horizontally along the base of the strip, one before the leftmost camera, one in each of the four gaps, and one trailing off the right edge, every arrowhead pointing right. Because the frusta aim away from the viewer while the arrows travel across the picture, the motion is perpendicular to the line of sight rather than along it. The sequence illustrates sideways camera motion parallel to the image plane, as when a scene slides past the side window of a moving car, in which the forward velocity component is zero.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Five identical wireframe camera frusta arranged in a horizontal row, evenly spaced, all with the same size and orientation
  - For each camera: a solid black filled dot at the bottom marking the projection center (optical center)
  - For each camera: a wide rectangle (image plane) standing above and behind the dot, drawn as a horizontal quad with visible top and bottom edges and two short vertical side edges
  - For each camera: four straight black edges running from the projection-center dot up to the four corners of the image-plane rectangle (the frustum/pyramid edges)
  - For each camera: two diagonal lines crossing the rectangle face from its upper corners down to its lower corners, so the pyramid reads as tilted up and away
  - Six thick red horizontal arrows along the base of the strip: one entering from the left of the first camera, one in each of the four gaps between cameras, one trailing off the right edge, all arrowheads pointing right
  - Implicit horizontal translation axis (camera baseline) running left-to-right through all five projection centers
  - Optical axis of each camera pointing away from the viewer (into the page), perpendicular to the red arrows
  - Scene points at two depths (a near point cluster and a far point cluster) in front of the cameras, used for the parallax/flow view
  - Projection rays from the moving camera center through the image plane to each scene point
  - Image-plane optical flow vectors (2D arrows on the image rectangle) showing lateral point displacement, longer for near points and shorter for far points
  - Camera-path trace line showing the lateral track of the projection center

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: one camera frustum is ~1.0 unit wide (image-plane rectangle X = 1.0), 0.45 unit tall (Y), and the apex sits 0.75 unit below and 0.6 unit toward the viewer (+Z) of the rectangle center — i.e. rectangle at z = 0 spanning x∈[-0.5,0.5], y∈[0.25,0.7]; projection-center dot at (0, 0, 0.6) rendered as a filled black sphere of radius 0.055. Four black edges join the dot to the four rectangle corners; two extra black lines cross the rectangle face from its upper-left and upper-right corners down to the lower-right and lower-left corners respectively (the visible X inside the quad, matching the source). Optical axis points along -Z (away from viewer). Five cameras placed at x-centers -4.8, -2.4, 0.0, 2.4, 4.8 (total strip span ~11 units), all identical in size and orientation, no scaling with position. Red arrows: thick horizontal shafts of color #FF0000, shaft thickness ~0.10 unit, total arrow length ~1.5 units with a triangular head ~0.45 unit long and ~0.30 unit wide, all pointing +X, lying at y = -0.55 (just below the apex dots). Six arrows at x-centers -6.4, -3.6, -1.2, 1.2, 3.6, 6.2, so one precedes the first camera, one sits in each of the four gaps, and one trails off the right edge. Line work is pure black #000000 at ~2.5 px equivalent; background white #FFFFFF. Scene points for the flow view: near cluster at Z ≈ -2.5 (6 points scattered within x∈[-1.5,1.5], y∈[-0.5,1.5]) drawn in blue #1F6FEB, far cluster at Z ≈ -8 (6 points over a wider spread) drawn in gray #888888. Image-plane displacement for a scene point at depth Z under lateral camera translation t_x: Δu = -f·t_x/Z with focal length f = 0.6 unit (distance from apex to image plane along the optical axis); therefore near-point flow arrows are ~3.2× longer than far-point arrows. Flow arrows drawn on the image rectangle in matching blue/gray, arrowheads pointing -X when the camera moves +X. Camera path trace: thin dashed red line along y = apex height, from x = -4.8 to +4.8.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "slideCamera",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Play lateral translation",
    "teaches": "The camera glides sideways parallel to its image plane — the optical axis never changes direction and the forward velocity component stays exactly zero, while the whole scene sweeps across the frame.",
    "loop": true
  },
  {
    "id": "lateralOffset",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Lateral displacement t_x (units)",
    "teaches": "Apparent image shift grows linearly with sideways camera displacement: doubling the baseline doubles the length of every image-plane flow vector.",
    "range": [
      0,
      4,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 0
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "View",
    "teaches": "Switching from the 3-D camera path to the projected image shows that the same rigid sideways motion produces depth-dependent flow: near points streak far across the image, far points barely move.",
    "options": [
      "3-D camera path",
      "Projected optical flow"
    ],
    "default": "3-D camera path"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the strip confirms the translation direction lies in the image plane and is perpendicular to the viewing direction, not toward or away from the scene."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The strip of cameras",
    "narration": "Five copies of the same camera stand in a row, each frustum aimed away from you while thick red arrows push rightward along the base. The pose never rotates — only the position slides sideways.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3-D camera path",
      "lateralOffset": 0
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Slide it sideways",
    "narration": "Drag the displacement slider: the active camera translates along the baseline with zero forward component, exactly like a scene sliding past the side window of a moving car.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3-D camera path",
      "lateralOffset": 2
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "What the image sees",
    "narration": "Switch to the projected view. Every scene point drifts laterally in the image, and the flow vectors all point the same way — opposite the camera motion.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Projected optical flow",
      "lateralOffset": 2
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Depth-dependent parallax",
    "narration": "Push the displacement to its maximum: the near points' flow vectors stretch far more than the distant ones, since image shift is t_x·f/Z. Lateral translation is what makes depth visible.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Projected optical flow",
      "lateralOffset": 4
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.55
}

NOTES: Build one frustum as a reusable THREE.Group (LineSegments for the 8 black edges + 2 face diagonals, a small black sphere or Points sprite for the optical center) and instance it five times along X. Use LineBasicMaterial with linewidth fallback — for the heavy stroke weight in the source, prefer thin tube meshes (CylinderGeometry along each edge, radius ~0.012) or Line2/LineMaterial from examples so thickness survives on all platforms. Red arrows: build from a flat ExtrudeGeometry or a BoxGeometry shaft plus ConeGeometry head, MeshBasicMaterial color 0xFF0000, unlit. Default camera: standard isometric axonometric pose via OrthographicCamera so the five copies stay exactly equal in size (no perspective shrink), zoom to fit the ~11-unit strip width at heightFraction 0.55. The animation should translate a single highlighted 'active' camera along the baseline while the five static ghosts remain as the strip; on loop, reset to x = -4.8. Slider and animation share the same lateralOffset state so dragging the slider scrubs the animation. In 'Projected optical flow' mode, fade the 3-D strip to ~15% opacity, bring an OrthographicCamera or a 2D overlay canvas to the front showing the image rectangle filled white with a thin black border, and draw the projected point positions plus flow arrows recomputed each frame from u = f·X/Z with the current t_x. Keep flow-arrow heads screen-space-constant so short far-point vectors remain legible.

Follow the interaction plan above.

The second image above is a screenshot of the PREVIOUS attempt's rendered output (not the source figure) — use it to see exactly what broke.
